@meta
id: 31-03
title: set becomes vim.opt
chapter: 31
type: lesson
skills: lua-config
par: 97
minutes: 6
vscode: editing settings.json, one JSON key per setting
open: init.lua

@brief
Here is the entire translation rule for boolean options, and it is as dull
as it looks:

    set number            ->  vim.opt.number = true
    set nonumber          ->  vim.opt.number = false
    set noexpandtab       ->  vim.opt.expandtab = false

`vim.opt` is a table that stands in for `:set`. Assigning to a field is
the same as running the `:set` command, and it happens at the moment the
line runs, in file order, exactly like a vimrc.

Two things people get wrong on their first day. There is no `no` prefix in
Lua — a disabled option is `= false`, written out. And `vim.opt.number`
without an assignment does nothing at all; Lua will not warn you, the line
just evaluates and is thrown away. A silently ignored line is the most
common bug in a hand-ported config.

The file in front of you is the start of an `init.lua`. The first option is
ported already. Port the remaining three from the vimrc section below,
in this order, keeping the same style:

    set relativenumber
    set expandtab
    set shiftwidth=2

Numbers are written as numbers — no quotes. Graded in vim: you are writing
the Lua, not running it.

@keys
| `G` | last line |
| `o` | open a line below and insert |
| `Enter` | in insert mode, keep going on a new line |

@hints
All three lines go at the bottom, under the option that is already there.
--
`G` then `o` opens one line below the last one. Stay in insert mode and
press Enter between the three lines rather than leaving and re-entering.
--
Do not open the line from a comment. `o` on a `--` line gives you a `--`
you did not ask for, exactly like `"` does in a vimrc.

@solution
    G o
    vim.opt.relativenumber = true<Enter>
    vim.opt.expandtab = true<Enter>
    vim.opt.shiftwidth = 2<Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Ninety-odd keystrokes and no thinking, which is the point: for plain
options the port is mechanical. Most people do this part of the migration
in one sitting with a substitution — `:%s/^set \(\w\+\)$/vim.opt.\1 = true/`
gets the booleans, and you fix the handful of lines with values by hand.

The reason `vim.opt` exists at all, rather than plain assignment, is that
vim options are not all scalars. Some are lists, some are maps, and
chapter 31-05 shows what `vim.opt` gives you that a plain variable could
not. For now: booleans are `true`/`false`, numbers are numbers.

@start init.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/init.lua
-- Ported from ~/.vimrc, one section at a time.

-- Display
vim.opt.number = true

@expected init.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/init.lua
-- Ported from ~/.vimrc, one section at a time.

-- Display
vim.opt.number = true
vim.opt.relativenumber = true
vim.opt.expandtab = true
vim.opt.shiftwidth = 2
